## Solution

We compute $I = \int_{[0,1]} x^2 \, d\mu_F$ where $\mu_F$ is the Lebesgue–Stieltjes measure induced by the Cantor function $F$.

**Self-similarity of the measure.** From $F(x/3) = F(x)/2$ with $y=3x$, for $x \in [0,1/3]$:
$$\mu_F([0,x]) = F(x) - F(0) = F(x) = \tfrac{1}{2}F(3x) = \tfrac{1}{2}\,\mu_F([0,3x]).$$
Thus $\mu_F$ restricted to $[0,1/3]$ is the pushforward of $\tfrac{1}{2}\mu_F$ under $t \mapsto t/3$. Likewise, using $F(1-x)=1-F(x)$ together with the scaling, one obtains $F((x+2)/3) = \tfrac{1}{2}(1+F(x))$, so $\mu_F$ restricted to $[2/3,1]$ is the pushforward of $\tfrac{1}{2}\mu_F$ under $t \mapsto (t+2)/3$. The middle interval $(1/3,2/3)$ carries no mass (since $F$ is constant there), so $\mu_F((1/3,2/3))=0$.

**Computing $J = \int x\,d\mu_F$ via symmetry.** The identity $F(1-x)=1-F(x)$ implies the measure is invariant under $x\mapsto 1-x$ (the pushforward $S_*\mu_F$ has distribution function $1-F(1-a)=F(a)$, hence equals $\mu_F$). Therefore
$$J = \int_0^1 x\,d\mu_F = \int_0^1 (1-x)\,d\mu_F = \mu_F([0,1]) - J = 1 - J,$$
so $J = \tfrac{1}{2}$.

**Computing $I$ via the self-similar decomposition.** Splitting over $[0,1/3]$ and $[2/3,1]$ (the middle contributes $0$):
$$I = \int_{[0,1/3]} x^2\,d\mu_F + \int_{[2/3,1]} x^2\,d\mu_F.$$
Using the pushforward descriptions with $x=t/3$ and $x=(t+2)/3$ respectively:
$$\int_{[0,1/3]} x^2\,d\mu_F = \tfrac{1}{2}\int_0^1 \left(\tfrac{t}{3}\right)^2 d\mu_F = \tfrac{1}{18}I,$$
$$\int_{[2/3,1]} x^2\,d\mu_F = \tfrac{1}{2}\int_0^1 \left(\tfrac{t+2}{3}\right)^2 d\mu_F = \tfrac{1}{18}\int_0^1 (t^2+4t+4)\,d\mu_F = \tfrac{1}{18}(I + 4J + 4).$$
Adding:
$$I = \tfrac{1}{18}I + \tfrac{1}{18}(I+4J+4) = \tfrac{1}{9}I + \tfrac{2}{9}J + \tfrac{2}{9}.$$
Hence $\tfrac{8}{9}I = \tfrac{2}{9}J + \tfrac{2}{9}$, i.e. $I = \tfrac{1}{4}J + \tfrac{1}{4}$. Substituting $J=\tfrac{1}{2}$:
$$I = \tfrac{1}{4}\cdot\tfrac{1}{2} + \tfrac{1}{4} = \tfrac{1}{8} + \tfrac{2}{8} = \boxed{\tfrac{3}{8}}.$$

